19. Common Anti-Patterns

Anti-Pattern 1:

Convert Everything to ToolError

Avoid:

except Exception as exc:
    raise ToolError(str(exc))

Problems:

Validation errors
Business errors
Dependency failures
Programming bugs

all become indistinguishable.

It may also expose sensitive information.

⸻

Anti-Pattern 2:

Use ToolError Inside Business Logic

Avoid:

async def service():
    raise ToolError(
        "Account not found."
    )

This tightly couples the service layer to FastMCP.

Prefer:

async def service():
    raise ValidationError(
        "Account not found."
    )

Then map it at the MCP boundary.

⸻

Anti-Pattern 3:

Return Generic Strings

Avoid:

except Exception:
    return "Something went wrong"

The model cannot determine:

What failed?
Should I retry?
Should I change parameters?
Should I ask the user?

Use structured application semantics.

⸻

Anti-Pattern 4:

Expose HTTP Errors Directly

Avoid:

except HTTPError as exc:
    raise ToolError(str(exc))

Normalize upstream errors first.

⸻

Anti-Pattern 5:

Retry Every Exception

Avoid:

except Exception:
    retry()

This may retry:

Invalid parameters
Permission errors
Business failures
Programming bugs

Retry only explicitly classified transient failures.

⸻

Anti-Pattern 6:

Expose Stack Traces

Do not return:

Traceback ...
KeyError ...
/internal/path/service.py ...

to the MCP client.

Keep detailed diagnostics server-side.

⸻

Anti-Pattern 7:

Rely Only on mask_error_details

Error masking is a useful framework-level protection.

It should not replace:

application classification
safe messages
correlation IDs
logging
retry policy

Treat error masking as defense in depth.

⸻

Anti-Pattern 8:

Use Global Retry Middleware Without Filtering

Avoid retry middleware that retries every exception.

A retry layer should distinguish:

transient upstream failure

from:

validation failure
business rule failure
authorization failure
programming bug

⸻

